     mmaakkeeffss [--bb _f_r_e_e_-_b_l_o_c_k_s] [--ff _f_r_e_e_-_f_i_l_e_s] [--MM _m_i_n_i_m_u_m_-_s_i_z_e]
            [--mm _m_a_x_i_m_u_m_-_s_i_z_e] [--OO _o_f_f_s_e_t] [--oo _f_s_-_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] [--SS _s_e_c_t_o_r_-_s_i_z_e]
            [--ss _i_m_a_g_e_-_s_i_z_e] [--TT _t_i_m_e_s_t_a_m_p] [--tt _f_s_-_t_y_p_e] _i_m_a_g_e_-_f_i_l_e _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The utility mmaakkeeffss creates a file system image into _i_m_a_g_e_-_f_i_l_e from the
     directory tree _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y.  No special devices or privileges are required
     to perform this task.

     The options are as follows:

     --bb _f_r_e_e_-_b_l_o_c_k_s
           Ensure that a minimum of _f_r_e_e_-_b_l_o_c_k_s exist in the image.  An op‐
           tional ‘%’ suffix may be provided to indicate that _f_r_e_e_-_b_l_o_c_k_s in‐
           dicates a percentage of the calculated image size.

     --ff _f_r_e_e_-_f_i_l_e_s
           Ensure that a minimum of _f_r_e_e_-_f_i_l_e_s (inodes) exist in the image.
           An optional ‘%’ suffix may be provided to indicate that _f_r_e_e_-_f_i_l_e_s
           indicates a percentage of the calculated image size.

     --MM _m_i_n_i_m_u_m_-_s_i_z_e
           Set the minimum size of the file system image to _m_i_n_i_m_u_m_-_s_i_z_e.

     --mm _m_a_x_i_m_u_m_-_s_i_z_e
           Set the maximum size of the file system image to _m_a_x_i_m_u_m_-_s_i_z_e.  An
           error will be raised if the target file system needs to be larger
           than this to accommodate the provided directory tree.

     --OO _o_f_f_s_e_t
           Instead of creating the file system at the beginning of the file,
           start at offset.  Valid only for ffffss and mmssddooss.

     --oo _f_s_-_o_p_t_i_o_n_s
           Set file system specific options.  _f_s_-_o_p_t_i_o_n_s is a comma separated
           list of options.  Valid file system specific options are detailed
           below.

     --SS _s_e_c_t_o_r_-_s_i_z_e
           Set the file system sector size to _s_e_c_t_o_r_-_s_i_z_e.  Defaults to 512.

     --ss _i_m_a_g_e_-_s_i_z_e
           Set the size of the file system image to _i_m_a_g_e_-_s_i_z_e.

     --TT _t_i_m_e_s_t_a_m_p
           Specify a timestamp to be set for all file system files and direc‐
           tories created so that repeatable builds are possible.  The
           _t_i_m_e_s_t_a_m_p is an integer value interpreted as the number of seconds
           from the Epoch.

     --tt _f_s_-_t_y_p_e
           Create an _f_s_-_t_y_p_e file system image.  The following file system
           types are supported:

                 ccdd99666600  ISO 9660 file system.
                 ffffss     BSD Fast File System (the default).
                 mmssddooss   FAT12, FAT16, or FAT32 file system.

     Sizes are specified as a decimal number of bytes and may use a multi‐
     plier, as documented in scan_scaled(3).  Two or more numbers may be sepa‐
     rated by an ‘x’ to indicate a product.

   ffffss ooppttiioonnss
     ffffss images have ffs-specific optional parameters that may be provided.
     Each of the options consists of a keyword, an equal sign (‘=’), and a
     value.  The following keywords are supported:

           aavvggffiilleessiizzee   Expected average file size.
           aavvggffppddiirr      Expected number of files per directory.
           bbssiizzee         Block size.
           ddeennssiittyy       Bytes per inode.
           ddiisskkllaabbeell     Name of a disk described in disktab(5).  A disklabel
                         with the information will be written at a machine de‐
                         pendent location in the image.  The size of the file
                         system image, the sector size and the bbssiizzee and ffssiizzee
                         parameters are inferred from the disklabel.
           eexxtteenntt        Maximum extent size.
           ffssiizzee         Fragment size.
           llaabbeell         Label name of the image.
           mmaaxxbbppccgg       Maximum total number of blocks in a cylinder group.
           mmaaxxbbppgg        Maximum blocks per file in a cylinder group.
           mmiinnffrreeee       Minimum % free.
           ooppttiimmiizzaattiioonn  Optimization preference: one of ‘space’ (default) or
                         ‘time’.
           rrddrroooott        A disklabel appropriate for a ramdisk will be built
                         and written at a machine dependent location in the
                         image.  The filesystem will be described by a FS_BS‐
                         DFFS partition ‘a’, with defaults offset=0,
                         fsize=512, bsize=4096, minfree=0 and density=4096.
           vveerrssiioonn       UFS version.  1 for FFS (default) or 2 for UFS2.

   ccdd99666600 ooppttiioonnss
     ccdd99666600 images have ISO9660-specific optional parameters that may be pro‐
     vided.  The arguments consist of a keyword and, optionally, an equal sign
     (‘=’), and a value.  The following keywords are supported:

           aallllooww--ddeeeepp--ttrreeeess      Allow the directory structure to exceed the
                                 maximum specified in the spec.
           aallllooww--mmuullttiiddoott        Allow multiple dots in a filename.
           aapppplliiccaattiioonniidd         Application ID of the image.
           bboooott--llooaadd--sseeggmmeenntt     Set load segment for the boot image.
           bboooottiimmaaggee             Filename of a boot image in the format
                                 “sysid;filename”, where “sysid” is one of
                                 ‘i386’, ‘macppc’, ‘powerpc’, or ‘efi’.
           ggeenneerriicc--bboooottiimmaaggee     Load a generic boot image into the first 32K
                                 of the CD9660 image.
           hhaarrdd--ddiisskk--bboooott        Boot image is a hard disk image.
           iissoolleevveell              ISO Level.
           llaabbeell                 Label name of the image.
           nnoo--bboooott               Boot image is not bootable.
           nnoo--eemmuull--bboooott          Boot image is a “no emulation” ElTorito im‐
                                 age.
           nnoo--ttrraaiilliinngg--ppaaddddiinngg   Do not pad the image (apparently Linux needs
                                 the padding).
           oommiitt--ttrraaiilliinngg--ppeerriioodd  Omit trailing periods in filenames.
           pprreeppaarreerr              Preparer ID of the image.
           ppuubblliisshheerr             Publisher ID of the image.
           rroocckkrriiddggee             Use RockRidge extensions (for longer file‐
                                 names, etc.).
           vvoolluummeeiidd              Volume set identifier of the image.

   mmssddooss ooppttiioonnss
     mmssddooss images have MS-DOS-specific optional parameters that may be pro‐
     vided.  The arguments consist of a keyword, an equal sign (‘=’), and a
     value.  The following keywords are supported:

           bbaacckkuupp__sseeccttoorr         Location of the backup boot sector.
           bblloocckk__ssiizzee            Block size.
